<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cohong</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cohong was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> of licensed Chinese merchants at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a> through which all Western maritime trade was conducted under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-system">Canton System</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>. The merchants were responsible for the conduct of foreign traders and the payment of duties. The system was abolished by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nanjing">Treaty of Nanjing</a> in 1842.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Its predecessor was the Baoshang system, set up in 1738, which licensed a number of Chinese merchants to trade with Westerners on condition that they helped to collect duties from them, so tying the merchants' interests to the government's revenue. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a>'s own foundation differ: the historian Immanuel C. Y. Hsu dates it to 1738, while John Phipps, author of the 19th-century Practical Treatise on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and Eastern Trade, credited the merchant Poankeequa with founding it in the 1790s. The Chinese merchants were known as hangshang and their foreign counterparts as yanghang.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Membership varied between five and 26 merchants authorised by the Qing government to trade with the West, above all in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, from offices called hangs. They worked under a revenue official known to the British as the Hoppo, a corruption of hubu, who held great power over their appointment and finances. Because of the low social status of merchants in the Confucian social hierarchy, a member was forced to pay bribes, levies and gifts to his superiors throughout his three-year term. Even so, the members' fortunes were among the largest in the Qing realm, and they worked to keep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> the sole site of official trade with the West.</p><p>From 1781 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> ran the Consoo Fund, a pool raised by a levy of 3% on the value of goods, which covered the debts of any bankrupt hong at the year's end and the exactions of officials. The levy at first applied only to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> but by the late 18th century covered 69 products.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from the New World paid for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a>, but when the British needed a substitute for silver they turned to opium from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. Despite the opium prohibition edicts of the Daoguang Emperor, the trade through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> came to centre on the drug. The Cohong did not distribute it within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, but its merchants arranged the small smuggling boats, called fast crabs or shifting dragons, that carried opium from Lintin Island to the warehouses of Canton. The British victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> ended both the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guild</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canton-system">Canton System</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cohong” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Canton System, formalised in 1757, restricted Western maritime trade to the single port of Guangzhou, where it was managed through licensed Chinese merchant houses, the <b>Cohong</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1757 the Canton <b>Cohong</b>, thirteen firms, held the sole right to trade with Western merchants, and tea came to make up ninety per cent of the exports leaving Canton.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li></ul></section>
